 So if W * T_t <= T_t + 2*T_p, then the sender is not fully utilizing the link; it sends W frames and then idles until ack arrives. So the throughput is (W * L) / (T_t + 2*T_p) because the cycle time is the time to send W frames and get ack for the first? Actually careful: In a sliding window protocol, the sender can send frames continuously as long as the window is not full. The window size W determines the maximum number of outstanding frames. The sender can send frames at rate R (the line rate) until it has sent W frames, then it must stop and wait for acks. The time to send W frames is W * T_t. After sending the first frame, the ack arrives after T_t + 2*T_p. So if W * T_t <= T_t + 2*T_p, then the sender will be idle after sending W frames until the ack arrives. So the total time to send W frames and get the ack for the first frame is actually the time from start of first frame to receipt of ack for first frame, which is T_t + 2*T_p. But during that time, the sender has sent W frames (if it sends them back-to-back). So the throughput is (W * L) / (T_t + 2*T_p). However, if W is larger such that W * T_t > T_t + 2*T_p, then the sender can keep sending continuously without idle, and the throughput is limited by the line rate R. So the maximum throughput is R (64 kb/s). So the throughput for a given W is: min( R, (W * L) / (T_t + 2*T_p) ). But note: The denominator is the time to get the first ack, which is T_t + 2*T_p. Actually, if W is large enough, the sender can send continuously, and the throughput is R. But we need to compute the exact throughput for each W.

